 1 0 2t0 2l321 264 321-264q1-1 1-4z m124 39l-34-41q-5-5-12-6h-2q-7 0-12 3l-386 322-386-322q-7-4-13-4-7 2-12 7l-35 41q-4 5-3 13t6 12l401 334q18 15 42 15t43-15l136-114v109q0 8 5 13t13 5h107q8 0 13-5t5-13v-227l122-102q5-5 6-12t-4-13z" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 850)"></path></svg></a></div></div></div><svg class="main-svg" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="401.6666666666667" height="251.66666666666669"><g class="hoverlayer"></g></svg></div></div></div></foreignObject></g><text class="draggable" name="text" x="50" y="25" r="0" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 50  25)" style="fill: rgb(244, 115, 93); font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 10px; font-family: normal;">in</text><text class="draggable" name="text" x="209" y="25" r="0" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 209  25)" style="fill: rgb(0, 255, 64); font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 10px; font-family: normal;">out</text><text class="draggable" name="text" x="159" y="63" r="0" rtemp="0" transform="rotate(0 159  63)" style="fill: rgb(255, 123, 0); font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 10px; font-family: normal;">CCCS</text><g transform="translate(379,37)" x="379" y="37" title="codeHTML" width="427" height="546" class="codeHTML" name="codeHTML" rot="0"><foreignObject x="0" y="0" width="222" height="200" style="height: 546px; width: 711.667px;"><div name="htmlCode" style="zoom: 60%; height: 546px; width: 711.667px;" code="&lt;div class=&quot;ch&quot;&gt;
    
    &lt;h1&gt;CCCS Current Mirror Amplifier - PyAMS Time Domain Analysis&lt;/h1&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Circuit Components&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;h3 style=&quot;color: #2980b9;&quot;&gt;Input Stage&lt;/h3&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;V1:&lt;/strong&gt; AC Voltage Source, Va=10V, Fr=100Hz&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;R1:&lt;/strong&gt; 100 Ω (input resistor)&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Diode:&lt;/strong&gt; Control element for CCCS&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;h3 style=&quot;color: #c0392b; margin-top: 15px;&quot;&gt;Output Stage&lt;/h3&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;X1:&lt;/strong&gt; CCCS (Current-Controlled Current Source), Gain=1&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;R2:&lt;/strong&gt; 100 Ω (output/load resistor)&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;h3 style=&quot;color: #27ae60; margin-top: 15px;&quot;&gt;Analysis Parameters&lt;/h3&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Type:&lt;/strong&gt; Time Domain&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Duration:&lt;/strong&gt; 0s to 0.02s (2 complete cycles at 100Hz)&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step:&lt;/strong&gt; 50 μs&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;UIC:&lt;/strong&gt; true (Use Initial Conditions)&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;Circuit Operation Principle&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-formula&quot;&gt;
        Input current: I_in = V(in) / R1&lt;br&gt;
        CCCS output current: I_out = Gain × I_in = 1 × I_in = I_in&lt;br&gt;
        Output voltage: V(out) = -I_out × R2 = -I_in × R2&lt;br&gt;
        Since R1 = R2 = 100Ω: V(out) = -V(in)&lt;br&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Voltage Gain = -1 (Unity gain inverter)&lt;/strong&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;Simulation Results&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-grid&quot;&gt;

        &lt;div class=&quot;ch-col blue&quot;&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Input Voltage V(in) - Red&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;ul&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Sinusoidal waveform at 100 Hz&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Amplitude: ±10V (peak-to-peak = 20V)&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Period: 10 ms (0.01s)&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;2 complete cycles in 20ms simulation&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Drives the input current through R1&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;

        &lt;div class=&quot;ch-col red&quot;&gt;
            &lt;h3&gt;Output Voltage V(out) - Green&lt;/h3&gt;
            &lt;ul&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Sinusoidal waveform at 100 Hz&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Amplitude: ±10V (same as input)&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;180° phase shift&lt;/strong&gt; (inverted relative to input)&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Unity voltage gain (|Av| = 1)&lt;/li&gt;
                &lt;li&gt;Perfect current mirroring with inversion&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card green-border&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2&gt;Conclusion&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;p style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;&gt;This circuit demonstrates a &lt;strong&gt;CCCS-based current mirror amplifier&lt;/strong&gt; with unity gain. The input AC source drives a current through R1 and the diode, which controls the CCCS. The CCCS replicates this current with a gain of 1 and delivers it to the output resistor R2. Since R1 = R2 = 100Ω, the output voltage magnitude equals the input voltage magnitude, but with &lt;strong&gt;180° phase inversion&lt;/strong&gt; due to the current direction convention. The circuit acts as a perfect inverting buffer, maintaining signal integrity while providing isolation between input and output stages. The simulation shows clean sinusoidal waveforms with no distortion, confirming ideal CCCS behavior.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class=&quot;ch-card&quot; style=&quot;background-color: #e8f8f5; border-left: 4px solid #1abc9c;&quot;&gt;
        &lt;h2 style=&quot;color: #16a085;&quot;&gt;CCCS Characteristics &amp; Applications&lt;/h2&gt;
        &lt;ul style=&quot;line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;&quot;&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CCCS:&lt;/strong&gt; Current-Controlled Current Source - output current proportional to input current&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gain = 1:&lt;/strong&gt; Unity current gain (current mirror configuration)&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Isolation:&lt;/strong&gt; Input and output circuits are electrically isolated&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Inversion:&lt;/strong&gt; Output is 180° out of phase with input&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Applications:&lt;/strong&gt; Current mirrors, signal isolation, current amplification, analog computing, transistor modeling&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Advantage:&lt;/strong&gt; Provides current buffering without loading the input source&lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;"> 
